<%# How the payment is written, next to the button that writes it.

    This needed a sentence, and the lack of one was reported as exactly the confusion it caused: *"I
    still don't understand the distinction between the two or the relationship between the two."* Three
    bare amounts beside a table that already totals to the same figure look like a second, competing
    summary. They are not. They are the **same money**, cut along the other axis - the Earns column adds
    up **per student**, this adds up **per reason** - and the two corners are the same number.

    So the split does not stand alone: the line above it says what it is. And the reason it is worth
    showing at all is the last clause of that line - `DistributeEarnings` writes **one transaction per
    reason**, so a student does not receive one deposit of their Earns figure, they receive three. This
    is the only place in the product that says so, and it is what their statement will list.

    Labels name what earned the money, not the subject: "Math grades", not "Math". On this page "Math"
    is already a column of letter grades, so the bare word would mean a grade in one place and a sum of
    money in another, twelve inches apart.

    No total line: the sentence above the block names it, and the confirmation names it again at the
    commitment. A fourth copy would be a fourth place to disagree. %>
<%= tag.div id: "finalize-breakdown", class: "mt-4" do %>
  <p class="max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
    Each student is paid in three deposits, which is how the total divides and what their statement
    will show.
  </p>

  <dl class="mt-2 max-w-xs space-y-1 text-sm">
    <% { attendance: "Attendance, including bonuses",
         math: "Math grades",
         reading: "Reading grades" }.each do |reason, label| %>
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-slate-600"><%= label %></dt>
        <dd class="tabular-nums text-slate-900">
          <%= number_to_currency(earnings.totals_by_reason[reason] / 100.0) %>
        </dd>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </dl>
<% end %>
